<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<domain-models xmlns="http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models
    http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models/domain-models_7.4.xsd">

  <module name="aiaccountant" package="com.axelor.apps.aiaccountant.db"/>

  <!-- The server's own copy of the visible conversation in one chat panel: every user message and
    assistant reply exchanged so far, in order, plus which record the page had open at each reply.

    WHY IT EXISTS. Two things read this row and need different amounts of it. A bare affirmation —
    «да», «направи го», «продължи», "same but the date +1 day" — means nothing on its own; it means
    something only next to whatever the assistant just proposed, which is why the LAST exchange is
    kept at all: AiRouteSelector requires the record on screen to canonically match that exchange's
    recordRef before a continuation is accepted, and every write still runs the full
    AiWriteAuthorisation chain exactly as an explicit instruction would. The Router Agent additionally
    reads the WHOLE list — see AiRouterContract — because resolving what "it", "the same one" or an
    unfamiliar word refers to routinely depends on more than one turn back.

    NOT A TRANSCRIPT SUBSTITUTE FOR THE MODEL. This is what the Router reads to route; it is not what
    the main agent reasons from — that is still Azure's own retained conversation behind
    previous_response_id. Nothing here is ever a proposal queue or an approval record.

    SCOPE. One row per (user, company, conversation), the same key AiWorkflowState and AiChatSession use
    and for the same reason: one person, one set of books, one chat panel. Appended to every turn and
    kept in full — never windowed to a fixed count and never aged out — because the user, not a
    timer, decides when a conversation is over: see the "Clear context" action, which starts a new
    conversationId and leaves this row behind unread.

    NOT AN AUTHORISATION. This row decides nothing by itself. It only lets a later turn's affirmation be
    RECOGNISED as continuing a specific proposal; whether that continuation is accepted is decided in
    Java, from the record on screen, and whether the resulting write is allowed is decided by
    AiWriteAuthorisation, unchanged. -->
  <entity name="AiTurnMemory">

    <!-- `column` is explicit because `user` is an SQL keyword and the generator refuses to derive a
      column of that name. Same as AiWorkflowState and AiChatSession. -->
    <many-to-one name="user" ref="com.axelor.auth.db.User" title="User" required="true"
      index="true" column="user_id"/>
    <many-to-one name="company" ref="com.axelor.apps.base.db.Company" title="Company"
      required="true" index="true"/>

    <!-- Which chat panel. Minted by the client, opaque here, only ever compared. -->
    <string name="conversationId" title="Conversation" index="true"/>

    <!-- The full exchange list, oldest first: [{userMessage, assistantReply, recordRef, at}, …].
      Each message is capped the same way the single exchange used to be (see AiTurnMemoryStore), but
      the array itself is never truncated here — only the amount of it any one caller is shown is
      bounded, and explicitly so; see AiRouterContract's history budget. Kept as one JSON document
      for the same reason AiWorkflowState keeps its target list as one: it is read and appended by
      one owner, once per turn, and splitting it into rows would buy a query nobody has asked for. -->
    <string name="historyJson" title="History" large="true"/>

    <!-- When the last exchange was appended. Read by AiRouteSelector's continuation check, which
      treats an exchange as eligible to be continued only while it is recent — see MAX_AGE in
      AiTurnMemoryStore — and unrelated to how long the row itself, or the history in it, is kept. -->
    <datetime name="lastTurnDateTime" title="Last turn"/>

  </entity>

</domain-models>
